  "query_preview": "..\nKEY MESSAGE 1...High pressure will result in tranquil \nconditions through Wednesday.\n\nBroad high pressure over the Great Lakes and Northeast slides\ntowards the Mid-Atlantic later today, then will be offshore\ntonight. A cold front approaches from the west on Wednesday.\n\nMostly sunny and mild today with highs in the low to mid 80s\nwith low humidity. Clouds increase somewhat tonight, and with a\nlight southerly flow, lows will be a bit warmer tonight.\n\nSoutherly pressure gradient tightens on Wednesday as that cold\nfront approaches from the west. A bit warmer and more humid on\nWednesday with highs in the mid 80s or so and surface dew points\nrising into the low to mid 60s. Showers and thunderstorms will\nstay to the north and west during the day.\n\nKEY MESSAGE 2...A cold front will cross the area Thursday \nbringing the potential for a round of showers and storms.\n\nMid-level ridging will begin to break down on Wednesday across \nthe region as an upper-low traverses portions of the Hudson Bay \ninto Quebec. On Thursday, a more pronounced shortwave will pass \nthrough the region. At the surface, a cold front will pass \nthrough the region Thursday. \n\nScattered showers and storms could begin moving into \nnorthwestern portions of the area as early as overnight \nWednesday night as the front begins to approach. PoPs through \nThursday morning are currently 20-40% and confined to the Lehigh\nValley, Poconos, and adjacent areas of far northern NJ.\n\nOn Thursday as the front makes its way southeastward through \nthe area, scattered showers and storms will likely accompany it.\nDespite somewhat impressive kinematics, most model guidance \nfeatures modest destabilization at best ahead of the front, with\nMLCAPE generally around 500 J/kg to 1000 J/kg. Discrepancies do\nremain with respect to timing of the frontal passage and \nassociated shower and storm chances. A slower front progression \nthrough the region could allow for a bit more destabilization \nthan is currently anticipated. All of this being said, the \nthreat for severe storms currently appears fairly low, but not \nzero. Any potential hydro concerns look limited by the \nprogressive nature of the front and storms. \n\nBehind the front, a drier and somewhat cooler airmass looks to \nsettle into the region for Friday. The forecast beyond Friday is\na bit more uncertain as some guidance hints that the frontal \nboundary may stall somewhere to the south of the region with a \nfew waves of low pressure riding along it. This will need to be \nmonitored as it could lead to somewhat unsettled conditions for \nthe weekend.",
